  "schema_version": 1,
  "source": "overnight-assemble-overlay",
  "summary": "The Federal Reserve held policy rates and still left a hawkish aftertaste. The U.S. 10-year finished at 5.05 percent, up 6 basis points, while the S&P 500 held near 7744 and the VIX sat at 17.52. Oil stayed firm, with WTI at 102.78 and Brent at 115.32, and gold near 4052. The chair talked tough on inflation at the Warsh news conference, and Treasury Secretary Bessent said another hike would not necessarily be a mistake even as he played down AI-financing worries. Those worries did not disappear: neocloud names sold off after Microsoft cut capex plans, banks shelved an Anthropic-linked data-center bond sale, and private-credit AI exposure drew fresh scrutiny. Japan's two-year auction saw firm demand ahead of Friday's widely expected Bank of Japan decision, with the 10-year at 2.867 percent. New Zealand inflation could not be verified, so NZ-specific new risk stays off. Next: the Bank of Japan, an expected ECB hold against French fiscal and energy risks, and whether the long end keeps backing up after the pause.",
  "type": "OVERNIGHT_ACCEPTED_PUBLIC_NEWS"
}
